Automated Web Hosting Using Shell Scripting
Project Overview
This project automates the deployment of a static website on an Ubuntu Linux server using a Bash shell script. The script installs and configures the Apache web server, downloads a website template, extracts the files, places them in Apache's web root, and restarts the service.

The goal is to reduce manual setup steps and make website deployment faster and repeatable.
Technologies Used
- Bash / Shell Scripting — automate deployment tasks
- Ubuntu Linux — server operating system
- Apache2 — web server
- curl — download the website template
- unzip — extract the downloaded ZIP archive
- APT — install required packages
- systemd — manage the Apache service
What the Script Does
1. Updates the APT package index.
2. Installs Apache2, unzip, and curl.
3. Starts Apache2 and enables it to start on boot.
4. Removes the existing /var/www/html directory.
5. Downloads the website template ZIP file.
6. Extracts the template into /var/www/.
7. Moves the extracted website directory to /var/www/html.
8. Restarts Apache2 to serve the deployed website.
Prerequisites
- An Ubuntu server or virtual machine
- A user account with sudo privileges
- An internet connection
- Inbound HTTP access on port 80 in the server firewall or cloud security group
Shell Script
Save the following script as deploy.sh:
#!/bin/bash
set -e

ZIP_URL="https://www.tooplate.com/zip-templates/2167_orbital.zip"

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y apache2 unzip curl

sudo systemctl enable --now apache2

# Replace the current website in Apache's document root.
sudo rm -rf /var/www/html

sudo curl -fL "$ZIP_URL" -o /tmp/website.zip
sudo unzip -o /tmp/website.zip -d /var/www/

sudo mv /var/www/2167_orbital /var/www/html

sudo systemctl restart apache2

echo "Website deployment completed."
Important: The script removes /var/www/html before deploying the new site. Back up any existing website files before running it. The extracted ZIP folder is expected to be named 2167_orbital; verify the archive contents if the template changes.

How to Run
Clone or upload this project to your Ubuntu server, then run:
chmod +x deploy.sh
./deploy.sh
The script uses sudo for administrative operations, so enter your password if prompted.
Verify the Deployment
Check that Apache is running:
sudo systemctl status apache2
Check the HTTP response locally on the server:
curl -I http://localhost
Then open the server's public IP address in a browser:
http://YOUR_SERVER_PUBLIC_IP
Make sure port 80 is allowed by your firewall or cloud security group. Do not use HTTPS unless you have also configured TLS.
